\lispPackageChapter{sigma/random}

The \texttt{sigma/random} package provides helpers for random sampling,
shuffling, and drawing from simple distributions.

\section{Macros}

\lispMacroSubsection{nshuffle}

The \texttt{nshuffle} macro randomly shuffles its argument in place.  It
expands to an assignment of \texttt{(shuffle \ldots)} back into the place.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(nshuffle \lispVar{argument})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(let ((xs '(1 2 3 4 5)))
  (nshuffle xs)
  xs) ; Returns the same elements in a random order
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{gauss}

The \texttt{gauss} function draws a sample from a Gaussian (normal)
distribution with mean \texttt{mu} and standard deviation \texttt{sigma}.  The
implementation uses the Box--Muller method, caching the second sample of each
pair between calls.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(gauss \lispVar{mu} \lispVar{sigma})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
\item[\lispVar{mu}] The mean of the distribution.
\item[\lispVar{sigma}] The standard deviation of the distribution.
\end{description}

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-argument}

The \texttt{random-argument} function returns one of its arguments chosen
uniformly at random, or \texttt{nil} if no arguments are given.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(random-argument \&rest \lispVar{rest})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(random-argument 'rock 'paper 'scissors)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{coin-toss}

The \texttt{coin-toss} function returns \texttt{t} or \texttt{nil} with equal
probability, like a fair coin toss.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(coin-toss)}

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-in-range}

The \texttt{random-in-range} function returns a random number in the half-open
interval $[\textit{lower},\textit{upper})$.  Either bound may be a sequence, in
which case the most extreme member of that sequence is used (\texttt{maximum}
for the lower bound, \texttt{minimum} for the upper).  If the lower bound is
greater than the upper, the bounds are swapped.  If they are equal, that common
value is returned.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(random-in-range \lispVar{lower} \lispVar{upper})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(random-in-range 0 10)     ; A number in [0, 10)
(random-in-range 0.0 1.0)  ; A float in [0.0, 1.0)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-in-ranges}

The \texttt{random-in-ranges} function, given one or more restricting ranges
each as a two-element list, returns a random number in a range common to all of
them.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(random-in-ranges \&rest \lispVar{ranges})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(random-in-ranges '(0 10) '(5 15)) ; A number in a common subrange
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-range}

The \texttt{random-range} function returns a two-element list
$(\textit{lo}\ \textit{hi})$ of random bounds within
$[\textit{lower},\textit{upper})$.  Without \texttt{:containing}, the two
bounds are independent samples ordered so that $\textit{lo} \le \textit{hi}$.
With \texttt{:containing} (a number or sequence), the result is chosen so that
the returned range covers that value or those values.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(random-range \lispVar{lower} \lispVar{upper} \&key
  \lispVar{containing})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{randomize-array}

The \texttt{randomize-array} function fills an array in place with values from
\texttt{(random \textit{argument-for-random})} and returns the array.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(randomize-array \lispVar{array} \lispVar{argument-for-random})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-array}

The \texttt{random-array} function returns a new array of the given dimensions
whose contents are randomized as by \texttt{randomize-array}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(random-array \lispVar{dimensions} \lispVar{argument-for-random})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(random-array '(3 3) 10) ; A 3-by-3 array of integers in [0, 10)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Generics}

\lispGenericSubsection{random-element}

The \texttt{random-element} generic returns a randomly chosen element from a
sequence, or \texttt{nil} if the sequence is empty.  Methods are provided for
lists and arrays.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(random-element \lispVar{sequence})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(random-element '(a b c d))
(random-element #(1 2 3 4 5))
\end{lstlisting}

\lispGenericSubsection{shuffle}

The \texttt{shuffle} generic returns a new container with the same elements as
its argument in random order.  The original is not modified; use
\texttt{nshuffle} to shuffle a place in place.  Methods are provided for lists
and arrays.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(shuffle \lispVar{container})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(shuffle '(1 2 3 4 5))
(shuffle #(a b c d e))
\end{lstlisting}
